  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nThe main feature, an upper trough will pivot east out of the \narea today which will take the precipitation with it. Strong \nnortherly flow along with cold air advection will dry out the \narea by this afternoon. Some model soundings and Hi-res \nguidance indicate precipitation that could briefly mix in or \nchange to snow across the highest elevations of the northeast \nmountains before ending this morning. Warm ground temperatures \nwill preclude any accumulations. Clouds will linger throughout \nthe day, especially across the mountains before the drier air \nbuilding in from the north finally erodes the cloud decks by \nlate afternoon. \n\nThe environment will decouple overnight and along with clear \nskies the perfect setup for a strong radiational cooling night \nwill take place. This will cause the already cooler temperatures\nin place, which will be well below seasonable for this time of \nyear, to plummet even further. Dewpoints will be 32 degrees or \nbelow across the board, therefore temperatures will have the \ncapability to drop to below freezing or lower, especially for \nthe mountains. For these reasons, the Freeze Watch has been \nupgraded to a warning for the entire area. \n\nThe same conditions are expected for Monday and Tuesday mornings \nwith the possibility of frost across parts of the lowlands and \nfreeze conditions in the mountains. If this Freeze Warning kills\nthe growing season then no headlines would be needed but if \nthere are areas that do not go below freezing then we may have \nto have some headlines going forward. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nQuiet weather and a warming trend are expected next week. High \npressure will remain in charge and anchored across the Ohio \nValley and Central Appalachians through much of the week, \nacting as a block against a closed upper level low meandering \nof the Mid-Atlantic and Carolina coast. This pattern will yield \na prolonged stretch of dry weather. Temperatures will be on an \nupward trend next week after a few cool days, the values will \nlikely climb into around seasonable or just above by midweek and\nbeyond. Steady height rises and modifying air will allow these \ntemperatures to moderate for the rest of the work week and the \nweekend. The next chance of precipitation in the medium range \nmodels is only in the Canadian NH which is forecast for next \nSaturday, however the other models do not agree with this \nsolution and keep us dry for longer.",
